families.purpose:
Pass/fail gates on the exact traded line. They cover:
- wrapper and US-situs class;
- USD venue and price units;
- product type (core, options-income, speculative bitcoin, information-only);
- sleeve mapping;
- same-index groups across wrappers, keyed on declared benchmark identity so same-index lines count once;
- feed integrity;
- IBKR orderability, W-8BEN and crypto or complex-product permission;
- data coverage.
Gates are never averaged into a score. They fail closed for buying and open for selling, and VWRA is never blocked by an owner-input or coverage gate.

Line view. Prices:
- the 30% investor-level US withholding on ordinary distributions from US-listed funds, always active. Dividends are read from yfinance's Dividends series only, de-duplicated; capital-gain distributions are excluded because they carry no withholding for a non-resident alien;
- the Irish 15% look-through on US dividends (and Luxembourg's 30%) wherever CST02 is missing;
- the fragility of any edge that rests on one tax rule;
- distribution friction.
Internal weights: TAX01 0.4, TAX03 0.4, TAX05 0.2.

Options-income view O (OPT = 100), applied only to options-income lines. Weights:
- upside given up (OPT01) 0.40, measured against the plain buy-write of the same underlying and against the line's own beta;
- net income after withholding relative to peers on the same underlying (OPT02) 0.25;
- beta-adjusted downside cushion and beta-matched net return gap (OPT03) 0.35.
OPT04 (premium regime) is context. The scales were rescaled in round 2 so no sub-score saturates. The gate: a line must score O at least equal to the O of the plain Cboe buy-write proxy for its underlying (BXM or BXN at zero fee), computed the same way. O ranks lines inside an owner-set options-income sleeve and never sizes or tilts the sleeve.

Judges the system on the owner's money against the strategic mix, with VWRA, 100% S&P 500 and 100% Nasdaq-100 as further comparisons, and decides when anything other than the mix may move money. It covers:
- point-in-time snapshots;
- redundancy and live IC, counted once per same-index group;
- the REC04 score gate and the REC07 mix test, both re-run against the 66.5/19/9.5/5 yardstick;
- execution, funding and behaviour logs;
- the news ledger, with one pre-registered primary forward test (REC08) and the rest labelled exploratory;
- agent calibration and source audit (REC09);
- the modelled-against-actual tax and cost ledger, with a shadow record for the new types (REC10).

pillars.definition:
Explorer rounds on:
- spot bitcoin ETF and ETP flows and assets (IBIT, FBTC, BTCW.L, IB1T.L and peers);
- rules from the SEC, CFTC, FCA (UK retail access to crypto ETNs), MiCA and VARA, and IBKR crypto-ETP permissions;
- incidents at custodians or exchanges, and stablecoin stress;
- buying or selling by bitcoin treasury companies, and miner economics.
Scores the bitcoin group from -2 to +2, labelled speculative, both absolute and against the world. Custody or sponsor incidents go at once to EVT05 and DUR02. Fee, creation-redemption and options-listing changes go to EVT02. Never resizes the bitcoin sleeve.

Explorer rounds on:
- distribution changes and their sources (option premium, ELN coupons, return of capital);
- strategy or overlay changes (ELNs against listed options, strike or coverage rules);
- the implied-volatility level, which sets premium income;
- flows and closures;
- new UCITS launches and fee cuts;
- the tax character of distributions (Form 1042-S, 871(k) designations).
Scores each ELG07 options-income group, by underlying and strategy, from -2 to +2 against its own underlying index. The groups are S&P 500 premium income {JEPI, JEPI.L}, Nasdaq-100 premium income {JEPQ, JEPQ.L, JEQA.L}, global premium income {JEPG.L, JEGA.L}, Nasdaq-100 BuyWrite {QYLD, QYLD.L, QYLU.L} and peers. Structural items go to EVT01, EVT02 and EVT05.

The agent scans held lines, shortlisted lines and same-index substitutes, in every wrapper, for:
- closure, merger, liquidation or trust termination;
- share-class conversion, delisting, ISIN or ticker change, share split;
- loss of the USD or primary-exchange line;
- a fund converting into a leveraged or single-stock product.
For US funds this includes SEC 497 supplements and N-14, N-8F and 8-K filings; for ETPs, issuer redemption notices.
Output 1, line severity: 0 none, 1 watch, 2 review (confirmed notice), 3 act. Two dated, cited sources are required, and a primary source for severity 2 or more. Penalty-only in the line view (50/40/25/0). Severity 2 or more blocks deposits at once and feeds S1.
Output 2, group score from -2 to +2, in news_log.csv. It comes from the severity of the group's held line, or its CST10-chosen line if none is held, through the map 0/1/2/3 to 0/-1/-2/-2. A group with an eligible substitute ready under DUR06 is scored one step less negative. A confirmed favourable action, such as a merger into a cheaper same-index line, scores +1.

The agent detects:
- issuers leaving the ETF business;
- changes of custodian, administrator or vault, and incidents at a bitcoin custodian or prime broker (each triggers a DUR02 re-check);
- swap, securities-lending and ELN counterparty downgrades, defaults or incidents;
- credit events at an ETP issuer;
- regulatory sanctions on an issuer, depositary or trustee.
It also keeps each issuer's closure and merger record. A finding applies to every line of that issuer or custodian.
Output 1, severity: penalty-only. Severity 2 or more blocks deposits at once, and an impairment of 5% or more of NAV triggers S1.
Output 2, group score from -2 to +2: severity 1/2/3 gives -1/-2/-2 for each group whose held or chosen line is affected. A group in which the incident hits most lines, such as a shared bitcoin custodian, gets -2 at severity 2 or more. A confirmed improvement, such as a second custodian added or segregation strengthened, gives +1.

The agent sets severity only when a primary source confirms a change. It watches:
- US estate tax for non-resident aliens: the US$60,000 exemption equivalent (IRC 2102, US$13,000 credit) and situs rules (IRC 2104);
- US withholding: 30% under 871(a) and 1441, the 871(k) exemptions, 871(m), 1446(f), W-8BEN rules, and any surcharge on foreign holders;
- the Ireland-US treaty rate of 15%, and the Irish fund regime;
- the Canada-US treaty estate credit, which needs Canadian residence and so does not apply to the owner;
- Canadian residency and departure rules (s.128.1, s.94.1);
- UAE personal and corporate tax, CRS and the crypto reporting framework (CARF);
- Indian tax on foreign portfolio investors;
- PRIIPs/KID access rules.
Severity 2 or more freezes sales (S6), except S1 and S8 switches that reduce US-situs exposure, and re-runs TAX01, TAX03, CST10 and FIT12. A confirmed change to the estate threshold updates FIT12's constants after the owner confirms it.
Group or wrapper score from -2 to +2:
- an adverse change gives -1/-2/-2 by severity for the groups or wrapper sets it hits. Examples: a US surcharge on foreign holders is -2 for US-listed lines in every group; a rise in Indian FPI tax is -1 for india;
- a favourable change confirmed by T1 plus T2 sources (for example a higher estate exemption, or a US-UAE treaty) gives +1, or +2 if material.

The agent watches IBKR changes for UAE clients:
- which IBKR entity holds the account, which also decides where USD cash sits for FIT12;
- LSE commission (US$1.70 minimum) and the US tiered or fixed plan;
- FX terms and the US$2 minimum;
- fractional shares for LSE and US lines;
- access to US ETFs under PRIIPs/KID rules, crypto-ETP permissions for LSE bitcoin ETNs, and W-8BEN renewal.
It also watches LSE and US exchange outages, the CBUAE peg stance, and UAE remittance and USD-wire fees. Confirmed changes update the allocator constants, ELG08 and the funding block.
Per-sleeve score from -2 to +2, mapped to the sleeves and venues each change affects: LSE UCITS lines, US-listed lines, the bitcoin sleeve (ETP or US ETF route) and the options-income sleeve.
- Adverse severity 1/2/3 gives -1/-2/-2. Examples: loss of US-ETF access is -2 for US-listed lines; withdrawal of crypto-ETP permission is -2 for the bitcoin ETP route; a higher US commission is -1 for US-listed lines.
- A confirmed improvement gives +1, for example cheaper commission, fractional LSE shares or new ETP access.

Readings:
- the 10-year minus 3-month Treasury spread: level, months inverted in the last 24, and the rise from its 24-month low;
- the 10-year minus 2-year spread;
- the real-time Sahm rule;
- the Chauvet-Piger smoothed recession probability (about 2 months' lag).
Pre-registered flag: on if the 10-year minus 3-month spread was below 0 in 3 of the last 6 months, or has risen 75 bps or more from its 24-month low after an inversion, or the Sahm rule is 0.50 or more, or the probability is 30% or more. On a FRED outage, the cached last-good value is shown with its age (missing beyond 45 days).

Readings:
- Moody's Baa minus 10-year: 20-year percentile and 3-month change;
- ICE BofA US high-yield, US investment-grade and EM corporate OAS: level and 3-month change only, because FRED serves just a 3-year window of the ICE series;
- the drawdown of the HYG/IEF total-return ratio from its 12-month high.
Pre-registered flag: on if Baa minus 10-year is at or above its 20-year 80th percentile or rose 50 bps or more in 3 months, or high-yield OAS rose 100 bps or more in 3 months.

Readings: the 3-month and 12-month change in the broad trade-weighted dollar; 3-month changes in EUR, JPY, GBP, CNY and INR against USD, as z-scores against 10 years; AED/USD against the 3.6725 peg. Two pre-registered flags:
- dollar flag: on if the broad dollar rose 4% or more in 3 months;
- peg flag: on if AED/USD deviates from 3.6725 by more than 0.2% on any day. It opens an EVT08 funding review and never skips a deposit.

Readings:
- the world reference's drawdown from its all-time high and from its 52-week high, mapped to states 0, -10%, -15%, -20% and -30%;
- core trend: VWRA month-end close against its 10-month average, and the sign of its 12-1 return;
- momentum-crash state: world 24-month return below 0 and 1-month return above 0. This state neutralises FAC06;
- breadth: RSP/SPY 6-month relative return, and the S&P 500 top-10 weight percentile.
Guardrails: at -15% or deeper, no non-structural sale; at -20%, no S5 bond rebalance, and the drawdown protocol note is shown. Information only; never a reason to sell (S0).

Classifies each line from its name, the universe type columns (leveraged, single_stock), yfinance category and legalType, and the prospectus strategy:
- core;
- options_income: covered call, buy-write, put-write or ELN premium income on a broad or sector equity or bond index;
- speculative_bitcoin: spot only;
- information_only.
Information-only covers:
- leveraged, inverse and daily-reset products;
- single-stock products, including single-stock option-income (TSLY, NVDY, MSTY, CONY);
- option income on a basket of a few single stocks or on other option-income funds (ULTY, YMAX);
- buffer and defined-outcome products;
- volatility-futures products, including short-volatility income (SVOL);
- futures-based crypto, bitcoin-linked option income (BTCI), and crypto other than bitcoin.
Information-only lines appear in a separate watch table and are read by NWS14. They are never bought and never count as substitutes. Any future bitcoin-linked product that is not spot is information-only, so FIT13's B cannot be bypassed. Options-income and spot bitcoin pass only into their own sleeves (ELG04).

(a) Membership. Each line gets exactly one group from its declared benchmark identity. The primary key is the issuer benchmark field and config/approved_equivalents.csv (to create). Active options-income lines are grouped by underlying and strategy from the prospectus. Groups:
- S&P 500: CSPX, VUAA, SPXS, VOO, IVV, SPYM, SPY;
- Nasdaq-100: CNDX, XNAS, EQQU, QQQ, QQQM;
- world: VWRA, FWRA, ACWD, VT, ACWI;
- spot bitcoin: IBIT, FBTC, BITB, BTCW.L, IB1T.L and peers;
- S&P 500 premium income: JEPI, JEPI.L (JEIP.L maps to it under ELG02);
- Nasdaq-100 premium income: JEPQ, JEPQ.L, JEQA.L;
- global premium income: JEPG.L, JEGA.L (JEGP.L maps to it under ELG02);
- Nasdaq-100 BuyWrite V2: QYLD, QYLD.L, QYLU.L.
(b) Return check. It confirms membership but never decides it. Monthly total-return correlation over 36 months, or at least 12 months for lines with shorter history (for example bitcoin ETPs since 2024 and JPM UCITS premium-income lines since 2024-10), must be at least:
- 0.98 within one exchange and currency;
- 0.95 across venues;
- 0.95 against the US twin or BTC-USD for active options-income and bitcoin.
It is also checked against an annualised return gap within the TER difference plus 50 bps. A failed check flags the line for review and is logged; it never splits a group with identical benchmarks. Weekly or daily correlations across London and New York are not used, because the two markets close at different times.
(c) Counting. Each group counts once in X, N, O, the fit view, REC03, and the trial counts of REC04 and REC08. Lines compete only in the line view.
(d) Splices and proxies. A splice needs 24 months or more of overlap, a monthly correlation of 0.95 or more across venues, and a gap of 50 bps or less. For a UCITS options-income twin, the US twin's gross history (before investor withholding) is spliced first: JEPI for JEPI.L, and JEPQ for JEPQ.L and JEQA.L. The Cboe proxy (BXM, BXN) is used only after that. BTC-USD is the history proxy for bitcoin. Options-income lines are never spliced onto their underlying.
Built-in test: CSPX, VUAA, VOO, IVV and SPY form one group, and so do VWRA and VT, and JEPQ, JEPQ.L and JEQA.L.

pillars.evidence:
Owner requirement 3: same-index lines count once. Measured with yfinance on 2026-11-13:
- weekly over 3 years: CSPX.L/VOO 0.93 (0.94 with a lag term), VWRA.L/VT 0.94, CNDX.L/QQQ 0.95, JEPI.L/JEPI 0.87, and JEPG.L/JEGA.L 0.89 for two classes of one fund;
- monthly over 36 months: CSPX.L/VOO 0.965, VWRA.L/VT 0.965, CNDX.L/QQQ 0.982;
- monthly over 25 months: JEPQ.L/JEPQ 0.985, JEPI.L/JEPI 0.976;
- monthly over 30 months: BTCW.L/IBIT 0.993;
- same exchange: CSPX.L/VUAA.L 1.00, VOO/SPY 1.00.
The round-1 weekly 0.98 test would have split every core group (quant audit). It was replaced by benchmark identity plus this monthly check.

pillars.definition:
Three parts:
- noise (scored): annualised standard deviation of daily return differences against the same-exchange group reference over 1 year, in bps. Cross-venue lines use the monthly gap instead, because the markets close at different times;
- quoted spread: median of 20 valid snapshots, taken Tuesday to Thursday at 14:00 London for LSE lines and 15:00 New York for US lines. A snapshot counts only if it is under 25 minutes old, bid is below ask, both sizes are above 0, and the spread is under 200 bps;
- premium or discount to NAV over 20 sessions, aligned to the close. Yahoo navPrice is never used.
The spread and premium parts are record only until 20 snapshots and a NAV history exist; then they share the pillar equally with noise. Weight 0.15 of CST.

pillars.evidence:
Owner requirement 3. Fallback figures on 2026-11-13, with true same-strategy twins:
- VOO: 3 + 30% x 1.07% = 35 bps, against CSPX 23.5;
- VT: about 53, against VWRA 24.6;
- QQQM: about 29, against XNAS 33.4 (inside the hurdle);
- JEPI: 35 + 238 = 273, against JEPI.L at about 55 (TER 35 + 15% on an assumed stock dividend yield of about 1.5%);
- JEPQ: 35 + 359 = 394, against JEPQ.L at about 45.
The round-1 'fails closed outside iShares-only groups' wording contradicted this fallback and was deleted (quant and data audits). The full form stays unverified until ter_history and the US rows exist.

pillars.definition:
Per equity group:
- the CAPE yield, its 20-year z-score, and the US excess CAPE yield (1/CAPE minus the 10-year TIPS yield);
- holdings' earnings, book, cash-flow and sales yields as a robust z-score against the world;
- the median 10-year return across at least 3 capital-market-assumption releases, shrunk toward the world's;
- the look-through Damodaran country risk premium.
An agent harvests CMA and CAPE numbers quarterly into config/cma.csv and config/cape.csv with URL and quoted span. Code fetches each page and confirms the number; rows that fail are never read. Weight 0. Each part is logged in REC02 and may enter REC04 only as a pre-registered variant.

The mean of three one-sided sub-scores over an expanding window of at least 10 years, using monthly USD returns, spliced:
- raw excess maximum drawdown against the world (k = 10 points);
- excess time under water: the longest peak-to-recovery spell minus the world's, with an unrecovered drawdown counted to date (k = 24 months);
- beta-adjusted shortfall over each stress window that ended at least 1 month before the date: Oct 2007-Mar 2009, Feb-Mar 2020 and calendar 2022 (k = 5 points).
Each component is logged for REC03. The world and anything calmer score 50. Weight 0.35 of RSK (14% of X).

Computed per same-index group:
- effective number of issuers (1 / sum of squared look-through weights, share classes merged);
- largest country weight, and half the sum of |country weight - world weight|;
- non-USD share and a hedged flag;
- half the sum of |GICS sector weight - world weight|;
- the top-10 weight and its percentile in the index's logged history;
- the look-through country risk premium.
Weight 0. The portfolio limits sit in FIT07 and FIT08.

pillars.evidence:
Owner requirement 4: scored on upside given up. Measured monthly to 2026-11:
- JEPI: U 0.61, beta 0.55;
- JEPQ: U 0.73, beta 0.66;
- QYLD: U 0.52, beta 0.51;
- XYLD: U 0.57, beta 0.61;
- SPYI: U 0.76, beta 0.70;
- QQQI: U 0.80, beta 0.74;
- BXM against the S&P 500 (2002-2026): U 0.56; BXN (2009-2026): U 0.44.
Scores: JEPQ (87 + 80)/2 = 84, QYLD (63 + 55)/2 = 59, which is 25 points apart; JEPI 68, XYLD 41. The round-1 scale (reference 1.0, k 0.15) put every line at 0.06-1.8 (quant audit). Covered calls sell the right tail (Israelov and Nielsen 2015; Whaley 2002).

Measured monthly to 2026-11, down-capture against beta:
- JEPI: 0.55 against 0.55, scoring 50 on part (a);
- JEPQ: 0.70 against 0.66, scoring 31;
- QYLD: 0.59 against 0.51, scoring 17;
- XYLD: 0.62 against 0.61.
BXM: down-capture 0.64 against up-capture 0.56, and a total-return gap of -3.75 points a year (2002-2026); BXN gap -10.2 (2009-2026). JEPI's protection roughly equals its beta, so it was not free (Israelov 2017). In round 1, part (a) saturated near 100 and rewarded low beta (quant audit).

pillars.definition:
For the spot-bitcoin group, counted once, in this order:
1. a stress replay through 2018, 2020 Q1 and 2022, and the US$ loss at the current weight and at 5% if the worst drawdown repeats;
2. the portfolio risk share at the current weight and at 5%;
3. 1-year, 3-year and since-2014 realised volatility;
4. drawdown from the all-time high and maximum drawdown since 2014;
5. 12-month and 36-month correlation and beta with the world reference and with the owner's mix.
Labelled speculative in every table. Any backtested return for bitcoin (REC07 variant (a)) is shown after the stress replay and labelled hindsight-selected. Weight 0; never score-sized. Feeds FIT13, RSK06, RSK07 and the owner note.

After the order and at target:
- IT plus Communication Services at no more than the mix + 5 points;
- total sector deviation from the mix at no more than 15 points;
- largest single-company look-through weight, share classes combined, at 7% or less.
Options-income lines count by their underlying's holdings. These limits are hard for score tilts and a warning for the owner's mix. Fails open for the core and closed for satellites when data is missing. Weight 0.

V_us = market value in USD of all US-situs holdings (ELG01 classes c and d: US-listed ETFs and US spot bitcoin trusts) at the latest close, plus any pending US order. USD cash at a US IBKR entity is shown next to it and flagged for adviser review, but not counted.
Bands:
- green below US$45,000;
- amber from US$45,000 to US$54,000: a warning in every weekly report, with months to US$60,000 at 7% a year growth; a US-listed line needs a 20 bps CST10 edge;
- red from US$54,000: no US-listed order may take V_us above US$54,000, and the same-group non-US line takes the order instead;
- above US$60,000 at a month-end: S8.
It fails closed for buying: no US-situs order is placed until ibkr_entity, w8ben_signed_on and positions_as_of are recorded in portfolio_v2.yaml, so that V_us and the US-entity cash flag can be evaluated. The cap value is read from config and changes only after an EVT06 change and owner confirmation. Weight 0; always binds, including on the owner's mix.

Since the start and over rolling 12 months: the actual portfolio's money-weighted return after all costs and all withholding actually charged, against shadows with identical deposits:
- the strategic mix as routed, on the cheapest UCITS lines (including 5% bitcoin under FIT13 and S7);
- VWRA only;
- 100% S&P 500;
- 100% Nasdaq-100.
Also shows the money-weighted minus time-weighted gap, and the worst drawdown against the world. The weekly display gives each holding's and each group reference's excess return over the mix and over VWRA for 4, 13 and 52 weeks, plus the 3-year information ratio, with the MAC09 drawdown protocol note. Display only.

Before launch and quarterly: Fama-MacBeth-style averages of cross-sectional Spearman correlations between scored pillars on the REC04 panel. Each ELG07 group is counted once. Intervals come from a block bootstrap with blocks of 24 months or more, and any pair at |rho| 0.85 or more on the lower bound is merged.
Mandatory pairs:
- FAC01/FAC03;
- the RSK01 components;
- CST02/TAX01;
- CST06/DUR01;
- NWS pillars against each other;
- NWS14 against FAC06;
- N against FAC06 and against the 1-week and 4-week group returns in the pack, because leads see recent moves and price-chasing could enter N.
Live IC of each scored pillar and view against forward 1, 3 and 12-month group-minus-mix returns, with the MAC10 state as an exploratory conditioning variable. The news part starts when REC08 has 52 weeks.

Re-runs the exact live tilt rule monthly under the frozen config hash, against the owner's mix. Before v3 cites any result, it is re-run against the new yardstick (world 66.5, S&P 500 19, Nasdaq-100 9.5, bitcoin 5, with S7) and on the cross-wrapper ELG07 groups, and the v2 figures are labelled 'v2 mix'. Settings:
- MONTHLY = 678.74, the US$2 FX minimum, venue commissions, the US$340 minimum order, whole shares, batching and the 2-of-12 cap;
- all constraints, including FIT12 and FIT13;
- US-listed proxies taken net of 30% withholding wherever the live line would be US-listed;
- both execution timings, sub-periods 2008-2014, 2015-2019 and 2020-2026.
It passes only if the rule beats the mix by 0.25 points a year or more in every sub-period under both timings, with a deflated information-ratio probability above 0.95. The deflation counts every trial, with N counted per same-index group (N_TRIALS 26, plus 1 for each new pre-registered rule). Only then may X or fit tilts route money.

For each order it logs:
- the line's return against the mix between signal and fill;
- fill minus mid at entry, the ladder rung, unfilled attempts and the MAC03 state;
- venue, commission and fractional fills;
- the EVT09 order-day choice, any delay and its group score, tested against realised entry cost;
- carried cash below the US$340 minimum;
- the funding cost (bank fees, the IBKR AED/USD rate against 3.6725, the FX minimum);
- the sessions used for S1, S2, S7 and S8 switches.
Once a line has 3 fills, the median of its last 6 replaces the modelled entry cost in CST10, taking 0.10 of CST weight from CST06.

Monthly:
- deposits on schedule, days from deposit to order, and every sale with its rule (S1-S9), sources, cost and hold counterfactual;
- a Brinson split of active return against the owner's mix, separating owner sleeve choice (bitcoin and options-income included), score or news tilts, line choice, wrapper choice (UCITS against US-listed after withholding) and cap trims;
- line regret, measured 12 months after each buy against the best same-index line.
Quarterly: a sleeve thesis log. For each sleeve beyond world it records cited structural facts and a verdict (intact, weakened or broken). The thesis log is record only.

Simulates the exact live routing (gap deposits of US$678.74, real costs, the US$340 minimum, whole shares, both timings) for the owner's mix against 100% world, 100% S&P 500, 100% Nasdaq-100 and standard blends, from 2003. Periods: 2003-07, 2008-14, 2015-19 and 2020-26. It is re-run with the new mix 66.5/19/9.5/5 as the base case. Variants:
(a) the mix with 5% bitcoin and the S7 trim to 4.5% (BTC-USD from 2014-09, 25 bps fee). It is labelled hindsight-selected, because bitcoin is chosen knowing its later success, and shown after BTC01's stress replay. Its return is never cited as evidence for the sleeve;
(b) the mix with an options-income sleeve of 5% or 10% in place of part of the S&P 500 or Nasdaq-100 sleeve (BXM from 2002, BXN from 2009, then live lines), after 30% withholding for US lines and none at investor level for UCITS. It is shown beside the owner prompt for an options-income target;
(c) each sleeve on its cheapest US-listed line after 30% withholding, against its UCITS line.
Information for the owner's mix choices. These are not trials and never gate the mix.

Logs every weekly NWS group score and every EVT severity and group score in runs/news_log.csv, one row per week, pillar and group:
- score and confidence;
- number of sources and of tier-1 sources;
- rounds, explorers, saturated flag and verifier result;
- model id, prompt hash, config hash and previous-row hash.
The log is frozen before Monday's LSE open; corrections are new rows, and there is no backfill. Tests run only forward, because agents scoring past news know what came next.
Primary test, pre-registered and the only gating trial (adds 1 to N_TRIALS): the weekly cross-sectional Spearman IC of the 4-week mean N (NWS plus EVT01, EVT02 and EVT04-EVT08 group scores) against forward 4-week group-minus-mix returns, with Newey-West t-statistics (lag 3). It is applied through the paper rule: an under-target sleeve whose 4-week mean N is 62.5 or more at 2 consecutive month-ends gets the deposit, within tilt limits.
It may route money only after:
- 104 weeks of log, which give about 26 independent 4-week observations;
- an IC t-statistic of 3 or more;
- beating the mix by 0.25 points a year in both halves under both timings, with a deflated probability above 0.95.
Exploratory, never gating, and labelled so: per-pillar ICs, the 13 and 26-week horizons (104 weeks give only about 4 independent 26-week observations), the hit rate of +/-2 scores, IC by MAC10 state, EVT09 and OPT04.

Per pillar and week:
- the share of cited URLs that resolve, carry a date inside the window and contain the quoted span. Below 95%, the pillar's scores read as missing that week;
- the blind second-lead agreement on a 10% sample (weighted Cohen's kappa of 0.4 or more). Below that for 8 weeks, the prompt is revised, which counts as a new trial;
- calibration flags: more than 25% of scores at +/-2, or scores correlating above 0.8 with the prior week's group returns (chasing price);
- the Brier score of EVT watch flags (did severity 1 become 2 within 13 weeks);
- the saturation curve (new distinct primary facts per round), explorers and rounds used against the caps, and every extra round a lead requested beyond the cap, with its reason;
- score drift after a model change.

Quarterly, from IBKR statements and Form 1042-S:
- the withholding actually charged on each line's distributions (expected 30% on US-listed lines and 0 on UCITS at investor level), any 871(k) exemptions, commissions, FX and fills, against the modelled TAX01, CST08 and CST10;
- a gap above 5 bps a year on a held line, or a withholding rate different from the model, opens EVT06 or EVT08 and re-runs CST10;
- the V_us path (FIT12) and the W-8BEN expiry date.
Also a monthly shadow record against three counterfactuals: every US-listed buy replaced by the best same-index UCITS line; the bitcoin sleeve replaced by the mix; the options-income sleeve replaced by its underlying. It also logs S7 and S8 trims and the realised up-capture and down-capture of options-income lines.

news_agent_design:
There are 25 news pillars: NWS01-NWS17 (market news) and EVT01, EVT02 and EVT04-EVT09 (structural events). Each gets its own lead agent. This replaces v2's single planner, shared explorer pool and 10 one-shot event agents in .claude/workflows/weekly-etf-review-v2.js. The planner, explorer and synthesiser machinery is reused, organised per pillar.

1. Week pack (code, Friday after run_v2.py). v2/packs_v2.py writes runs/{week}/news_packs/{ID}.md for each pillar with:
- the pillar's scope and its fund groups from config/news_map.yaml (to create). The groups are the 28 buckets in config/buckets.txt plus bitcoin and the ELG07 options-income groups (by underlying and strategy). Each is mapped to ELG07 same-index groups and owner sleeves with frozen weights m. EVT08 also maps to the venue sets (LSE UCITS, US-listed, bitcoin ETP route, US bitcoin route);
- each group's 1-week and 4-week return against the mix, as a z-score on 52-week volatility; moves with |z| above 2 are marked unexplained;
- the held and shortlisted lines (UCITS, US-listed, LSE ETPs) and their groups;
- MAC01-MAC10 readings and any flag that turned this week;
- the EVT09 calendar for the window;
- last week's scores, open threads and watch flags;
- the pillar's source whitelist and primary sources.
The window runs Saturday to Friday close of the ISO week. Older material is allowed only when labelled as background, and background never sets a score.

2. Lead agent per pillar. It reads the pack, runs 1-2 dated headline searches and chooses its own topics. It must seed a topic for each of these:
(a) every unexplained group move;
(b) every held or owner-mix group in its map, at least as a 'no material news' check;
(c) every carried-over open thread or EVT watch flag;
(d) every scheduled event in the window;
(e) every MAC flag change mapped to it: MAC01, MAC04 and MAC08 to NWS01; MAC02 and MAC04 to NWS02; MAC05 to NWS11 and EVT08; MAC07 to NWS12 and NWS13.
Some pillars have fixed checklists:
- EVT01 covers every held line and its same-index substitutes;
- EVT02 covers every blank TER to seed;
- EVT06 covers each listed tax rule;
- NWS15 and NWS16 cover the shortlisted bitcoin and options-income lines.
Topics must be specific: a release, a decision, a company, a filing or a country event. The lead merges overlaps and sends one explorer per topic.
Explorer count is the lead's decision above a floor. The floor is 1 + A, where A is an activity index from 0 to 4 scoring 1 point each for an unexplained move, a mapped MAC flag change, an open thread, and a held line in scope. The lead may raise the count to the cap of 6 per round. A quiet scope (A = 0) may run 1 explorer but must log 3 dated source checks. Each later round's count is again the lead's choice, up to 6.

3. Explorer rounds. Each explorer returns facts {claim, date, source, URL, verbatim quoted span, tier, fact or opinion}, the groups affected with a provisional score from -2 to +2, and new threads it could not cover. Tiers: T1 is a primary source; T2 is top-tier press (Reuters, Bloomberg, FT, WSJ, Nikkei); T3 is everything else.
After each round the lead runs a saturation test. A pillar is saturated only when all four hold:
(i) the last round changed no group score by 1 or more and no EVT severity;
(ii) fewer than 20% of the round's facts are new distinct T1 or T2 facts;
(iii) no open thread passes the relevance test, meaning none could change a group score by 1 or more, an EVT severity, a gate, FIT12 or FIT13;
(iv) every mapped group has at least 2 dated sources or an explicit null check.
If it is not saturated, the lead sends explorers only for the threads that pass (iii), with no repeats of topics already asked. The lead decides when to stop, within these caps:
- 4 rounds per pillar, or 5 for an EVT pillar holding an unconfirmed severity-2 candidate;
- 6 explorers per round and 18 per pillar;
- a lead may request one extra round beyond the cap, with a logged reason audited in REC09;
- a workflow budget of 450 agents, with priority to EVT pillars, then pillars touching held sleeves, then higher A.
A pillar stopped by a cap is logged as unsaturated, its confidence is capped at low, and any score is capped at |1|. Every task skipped at the cap is logged.

4. Cross-pillar synthesiser, after each global round and at the end. It:
- de-duplicates facts by URL and event key, and gives each fact one owning pillar, so one event is scored once in N (the news equivalent of counting same-index lines once). Other pillars may reference it but not score it again;
- flags contradictions and names cross-topic chains, for example oil supply shock, then breakevens, then real yields, then the Nasdaq-100;
- routes threads to the pillar that owns them; structural facts found by NWS explorers go to the right EVT pillar;
- runs at most one adjudication round of up to 6 explorers.
After synthesis, a blind verifier agent re-checks every +/-2 score and every EVT severity of 2 or more. The score survives only if the verifier finds no missing counter-fact and no unconfirmed source.

5. Scoring per fund group, for all 25 pillars on -2..+2.
NWS leads score each mapped group for expected USD return over the next 1-3 months:
- most groups: against the world;
- options-income groups: against their underlying;
- global_equity and bitcoin: also an absolute score, logged only.
Scores judge policy, fundamentals and flows, not price moves alone. 0 is the default, and most groups score 0 or +/-1 in most weeks. Rules:
- each score carries a confidence (high 1, medium 0.67, low 0.33), a rationale of 2-4 sentences, what would prove it wrong, and fact ids;
- |1| needs 2 independent dated sources inside the window;
- |2| needs a T1 source plus a T2 report and a concrete, material development;
- opinion never supports a 2, and T3 can open a thread but never supports a score alone;
- NWS14 uses its pre-registered contrarian sign.
Calibration anchors sit in the prompt; for example, a 25 bp surprise cut is +1 for rate-sensitive groups, not +2.
EVT leads write two outputs:
(1) severity 0-3 per line or issuer. Severity 2 or more needs a T1 source with the quoted text. EVT02's cheaper-substitute trigger and EVT07's restricted-market rows are confirmed by code;
(2) a group or sleeve score on -2..+2 under the same source and confidence rules, from the pre-registered map. Adverse severity 1, 2 or 3 gives -1, -2 or -2 for the groups whose held or CST10-chosen line, or whose whole exposure, is affected; EVT01 is one step less negative when a ready substitute exists. A confirmed favourable event gives +1: a fee cut, a cheaper same-index substitute, a new UCITS twin, an access improvement, lower index concentration, or a lifted sanction. A material favourable rule change confirmed by T1 plus T2 gives +2. Otherwise the score is 0. EVT09 scores only -1 or 0, for an order-window conflict.
News view per group: N_g = 50 + 12.5 x sum_p(m_pg x c_pg x s_pg) / sum_p(m_pg x c_pg), over the 17 NWS pillars and EVT01, EVT02 and EVT04-EVT08; EVT09 is excluded as an execution score. N ranges from 25 to 75. Groups no pillar touched read 50 and are flagged 'not covered'. The 4-week mean of N is the one primary signal tested in REC08.

6. Code checks before anything is stored. Every URL is fetched (with redirects followed) and must return HTTP 200, its date must fall inside the window (otherwise it is relabelled background), and the verbatim quoted span must be present on the page. Facts that fail are dropped. A score left with fewer than 2 valid sources becomes 0 with low confidence. Paywalled facts need a second source, and social media counts only from official accounts. Before any severity of 2 or more opens a gate, S1, S6 or S8, code confirms the quoted primary-source text and the owner confirms the action. Agents never write numbers into scored config; TER seeds from EVT02 are code-checked against the KID before entering ter_history.

7. Logging. Each pillar writes:
- runs/{week}/news/{ID}.json: facts with quotes, group scores, severities, rounds, explorers, saturation, verifier result and agent count;
- a digest under 300 words in {ID}.md;
- for EVT pillars, also runs/{week}/events/{ID}.json.
Code then appends rows to two files, both created before the first v3 run:
- runs/news_log.csv: one row per week, pillar and group, for all 25 pillars, with the REC08 fields;
- runs/news_facts.csv: fact id, URL, date, tier, verbatim quote, quote hash and owning pillar.
Both are append-only and hash-chained, and they are frozen before Monday's LSE open, with the hash copied into plan_log.csv. A correction is a new row with a reason and is excluded from REC08's IC. REC09 audits sources, saturation, extra rounds and agreement between leads.

8. What news may do.
- EVT severity 2 or more blocks deposits to the affected lines at once, and opens S1, S6 or S8 after primary-source confirmation. EVT06 can update FIT12's cap after owner confirmation.
- NWS and EVT group scores and N are display only next to the line, X, O and fit views. They are never a reason to sell (S0), never size bitcoin or options-income, and never move deposits until REC08's primary test passes (104 weeks or more of live log), followed by the REC04 standard.
- There is no historical backtest of news, because agents know later outcomes.
- The weekly committee in weekly-etf-review-v2.js keeps its role, but cites ledger rows instead of free text. Leads may see holdings, for relevance, but not the owner's gains or losses.

scoring_method:
1. Instrument and grouping. Line pillars (ELG, CST, TAX, DUR, line EVT) are computed on the exact traded line: an LSE USD UCITS line, a US-listed line or an LSE bitcoin ETP. Every line belongs to one ELG07 group, keyed on declared benchmark identity across wrappers and confirmed by a monthly return check (0.95 across venues, 0.98 within one exchange). X, N, O and the fit view are computed once per group on its reference series, spliced under ELG07. Every count, IC, redundancy test and REC04/REC08 trial count treats a group as one. Same-index lines differ only in the line view.

2. Gates first. A failing line is still scored and shown, but cannot receive money. Gates fail closed for buying and open for selling, and VWRA is exempt from ELG08 and ELG09. The gates:
- ELG01-ELG05, ELG07-ELG09 (a blank TER fails ELG09);
- DUR02 for gold ETCs, bitcoin products and the ELN counterparty limit;
- CON04 above 5% (missing until its file exists), and CON05 flag 1;
- FIT07 and FIT08 (for tilts), FIT11, FIT12 (red band, or missing entity, W-8BEN or positions_as_of for a US-situs order), FIT13 (5% cap, US$340 minimum);
- EVT01, EVT02 or EVT05 at severity 2 or more, and EVT07 at severity 3;
- for an options-income line, O below the O of its plain buy-write proxy.
Information-only types (ELG03) are shown in a separate watch table.

3. Transform (unchanged from v2): s = 50 + 50 x tanh(d x (x - ref) / k), in natural units, never universe percentiles. One-sided pillars use s = 50 - 50 x tanh(max(0, d x (x - ref)) / k) and never exceed 50: RSK01 (each component), RSK03, RSK05, FIT10, TAX03, DUR01's flow part and line EVT. The k scales are frozen in config/pillar_scales.yaml. New scales:
- TAX01 for US-listed lines: k = 15 bps;
- OPT01(a): capture against the buy-write proxy, k = 0.30; OPT01(b): up-capture minus beta, k = 0.10;
- OPT02: net income against the peer median, k = 3 points;
- OPT03(a): down-capture minus beta, k = 0.10; OPT03(b): beta-matched net gap, k = 2 points;
- news scores: linear, capped at +/-2.

4. One beta (FAC05; options-income lines also get a beta to their underlying) and one factor set (Ken French Developed 5 factors plus momentum, CMA orthogonalised to HML), as in v2.

5. Five views, with no cross-view composite:
- Line view, per same-index group: CST 50, TAX 24, DUR 19, EVT 7 = 100. Lines are ranked against the group's median eligible line; single-line groups use absolute anchors (TER 10 bps, AUM US$1bn). CST10, at weight 0, picks the line, using its fallback (TERnow + TAX01 + entry) wherever CST02 is missing.
- Exposure view X, per equity group, with the world = 50: EXR 20, FAC 40, RSK 40, identical to v2. EXR06 is missing (not built) until its probe passes, so EXR is scored on EXR08 with coverage c. REC04 must be re-run in this state against the new mix before its result is cited for v3. Bitcoin is excluded. Options-income groups get X on their own history, for information only.
- Options-income view O, per options-income line: OPT01 0.40, OPT02 0.25, OPT03 0.35. The reference for each part is: OPT01 against the plain buy-write proxy and beta; OPT02 against the peer median; OPT03 against beta and a beta-matched mix.
- Fit view, with the current portfolio = 50 (the owner's mix at target until positions exist): FIT04 0.40, FIT05 0.20, FIT06 0.20, FIT10 0.20 for equity groups; FIT05, FIT06 and FIT10 at 1/3 each for gold, bonds, bitcoin and options-income.
- News view N, per group: 50 + 12.5 x the mapping- and confidence-weighted mean of the group scores from NWS01-NWS17 and EVT01, EVT02 and EVT04-EVT08 (range 25-75). EVT09 is logged but excluded.

6. Weights inside families:
- CST: CST01 0.30, CST02 0.35, CST04 0.15 (noise only until 20 snapshots and NAV history exist), CST06 0.15, CST08 0.05. From 3 fills, REC05's realised cost takes 0.10 from CST06.
- TAX: TAX01 0.4, TAX03 0.4, TAX05 0.2. TAX01's fund-level part is inactive where CST02 exists, and its non-US look-through component is recorded at 0 until the treaty table is verified. Its investor-level part (30% for US-listed lines) is always active, and UCITS lines read 0 bps on it. In a group made only of UCITS lines with CST02 present, TAX03 and TAX05 are renormalised to 2/3 and 1/3.
- DUR: DUR01 0.6, DUR06 0.4 (DUR02 is a gate).
- Line EVT: EVT01, EVT02 and EVT05 severities in equal thirds, penalty-only at 50/40/25/0.
- EXR: EXR06 0.4, EXR08 0.6.
- FAC: FAC01 0.12, FAC02 0.30, FAC03 0.28, FAC04 0.30.
- RSK: RSK01 0.35, RSK03 0.35, RSK05 0.30.
Caps: the value cluster is 24.8% of X (cap 25%) and the drawdown cluster 14% (cap 15%).

7. Weight 0 (gate, context, constraint, record or derived): all ELG, MAC and REC pillars; BTC01; CON02, CON04, CON05; CST10; DUR02; EXR02, EXR03, EXR10, EXR11; FAC05, FAC06; RSK06, RSK07; OPT04; FIT01, FIT07, FIT08, FIT11, FIT12, FIT13; EVT04 and EVT06-EVT09 in the line view.

8. Verification and missing data.
- A pillar with an unverified source has weight 0 until a probe passes and is logged in CHANGELOG. Unverified in round 2: NWS14, ELG08, CST02, CST10 (full form), TAX03, DUR02, DUR06, EXR06, EXR10, CON04, FIT01, FIT12, FIT13, and REC01-REC03, REC05, REC06 and REC08-REC10.
- Unverified constraints and gates fail closed for buying: FIT12 blocks US-situs orders, FIT13 reads the bitcoin and options-income targets as 0, DUR02 blocks gold and bitcoin lines, and ELG08 blocks unconfirmed lines. The exception is CON04, which is listed as missing because it has no rows.
- A missing pillar drops out, and the family score becomes 50 + c x (mean - 50), where c is the share of the family's weight with data. Nothing is filled with 50.
- If the group reference lacks a pillar, the pillar is missing for the whole group.
- For news, an unsaturated pillar is capped at |1| and low confidence; a pillar failing the REC09 audit reads missing for that week; a verified null check reads 0.
- FRED is fetched one series per request with curl --http1.1, falling back to the cached last-good value with an age flag (missing beyond 45 days). Cboe files are fetched with curl -L.
- Missing pillars are listed weekly.

9. Point in time. Stress windows count only once they have ended plus 1 month. Ken French, FRED and BIS data use release lags. News scores count only from the ledger row frozen before Monday's open. MAC series that are revised need ALFRED vintages before any historical test.

10. Smoothing. Monthly line decisions use the median of 4 weekly readings. X for targets is the mean of the last 3 month-ends. N for testing is the 4-week mean. Weekly values are display only.

11. Promotion. A view may move money only when its exact pre-registered rule passes REC04 against the owner's mix. For news, REC08's single primary test must pass first; other news ICs are exploratory. Each new rule adds 1 to N_TRIALS. Every k, threshold, weight, MAC flag rule, news prompt and cap band is frozen under one config hash, and any change is a new trial.

12. Built-in checks. The run fails loudly if any of these breaks:
- the world scores 50 on every X family;
- FIT04 = 0 +/- 0.1% with zero tilts;
- each same-index group appears exactly once in every count and IC;
- CSPX, VUAA, VOO, IVV and SPY form one group, VWRA and VT one, and JEPQ, JEPQ.L and JEQA.L one;
- money-market lines fail ELG04, and leveraged, single-stock, SVOL, YMAX, ULTY and BTCI fail ELG03;
- the US buy-candidate count is 145;
- CST10 for a US-listed line includes 30% of its trailing ordinary distribution yield (Dividends only, de-duplicated); in fallback mode it reproduces VOO 35, VT 53, JEPI 273 and JEPQ 394 bps from the 2026-11-13 inputs, with JEPI compared to JEPI.L and JEPQ to JEPQ.L;
- a US-listed line within 10 bps of the best non-US line is never chosen;
- FIT12's V_us equals the sum over ELG01 classes c and d;
- no order takes V_us above US$54,000 or bitcoin above 5%, and no month-end leaves B above 5.0% without an S7 order;
- no order or split remainder is below US$340;
- OPT01 for JEPQ and QYLD is at least 15 points apart, and no OPT sub-score sits above 90 or below 10 for more than half the options-income lines (saturation check);
- no information-only line can receive an order;
- every news and EVT group score has 2 valid sources or is 0;
- the news-log hash precedes the month's first fill;
- the config hash matches the one REC04 and REC08 tested.

sell_rules:
Default: hold, and steer with new money. Deposits fix drift before any sale. Every sale is logged with its rule, sources, cost and hold counterfactual (REC06), and needs the owner's confirmation, except S7, which the owner pre-confirmed by setting the 5% cap. Under UAE Branch A a sale has no tax cost; the costs are commission, spread and FX.

S0. Never a reason to sell:
- price trend or momentum (MAC09, FAC06);
- any score or rank (X, fit, O, N), any NWS score, or any EVT group score;
- drawdown depth, volatility, any MAC reading or MAC10 state;
- bitcoin drawdowns or crypto volatility (BTC01);
- an options-income line lagging its index in a rally, or cutting its distribution;
- the REC06 thesis verdict.
Evidence: weekly top-5 rotation returned 5.71% and monthly 8.68%, against 11.85% for holding (2015-2026); selling the core on its own downtrend returned 10.02% against 11.85%; REC04 tilts lose to the mix.

S1. Structural exit of a line, completed within one month of primary-source confirmation. Buying stops at once. Triggers:
- EVT01 at severity 2 or more;
- a gate turning to fail: ELG01, ELG02, ELG03 (including reclassification to information-only), ELG04, ELG08 confirmed by the owner, or DUR02 (custody or ELN counterparty failure);
- ELG05 stale for more than 7 trading days, confirmed;
- EVT04 or EVT02 moving the line out of its group or strategy;
- EVT05 or EVT07 impairing 5% or more of NAV, including bitcoin custody loss or an ETP issuer default event.
Action: switch the whole position to the group's best eligible substitute (DUR06, lowest CST10), never into cash. A US-listed substitute is allowed only within FIT12 headroom; otherwise the substitute is UCITS or an LSE ETP. For bitcoin with no eligible bitcoin line, sell into the most underweight sleeve. Positions above 10% of the target line's median daily traded value are split across sessions (CST06), and the switch waits while the aligned NAV discount is above 2%, for 60 days at most.

S2. Cost or durability switch. First stop buying and send new money to the substitute. Sell only if all three hold:
(a) the held line's CST10 exceeds the best eligible same-group line's by 15 bps a year or more at 2 consecutive quarterly checks;
(b) 10 years of the saving exceed 3 times the round trip;
(c) less than half of the saving rests on one tax rule (TAX03).
This covers a US-listed line made dearer than a UCITS line by withholding or fee changes (REC10 or EVT06 evidence), for example a US options-income line against a new UCITS twin. A durability flag (DUR01 small with outflows, or EVT05 severity 1) stops buying; a switch follows only if the flag holds for 2 monthly runs and a substitute exists.

S3. Drift beyond limits (score-tilt sleeves, band maxima, FIT07, FIT08, RSK06, RSK07, and the options-income limit in FIT13). Stop buying the sleeve. Sell only if FIT01 shows that more than 12 months of deposits would be needed to cure it. Then sell down to the limit only, at most once a quarter, and buy the most underweight sleeve in the same session. Bitcoin is governed by S7, not S3.

S4. Removing a sleeve from the mix (the owner sets its target to 0, including bitcoin or options-income). Freeze it: no new money and no sale. Deposits dilute it. Bitcoin is still trimmed under S7 whenever it is above 5%.

S5. Bond-share rebalance (only if D is above 0). Once a year, never while the world is more than 20% below its high (MAC09).

S6. Tax or legal change for the owner (EVT06 at severity 2 or more). Freeze all sales until an adviser review and a re-run of TAX01, TAX03, CST10 and FIT12. S1 and S8 switches that reduce US-situs exposure still go ahead, and so does S7.

S7 (revised). Speculative bitcoin cap at 5%, a true cap.
- The check runs at every month-end, after that month's deposit has been routed (no bitcoin buy happens while B is 5% or above, so new money dilutes B first).
- If B is still above 5.0%, sell the bitcoin group down to 4.5% of the portfolio in one order, on the cheapest-to-sell held bitcoin line. The proceeds are routed by FIT01 gaps in the same session.
- Each trim is at least 0.5% of the portfolio, so its US$1.70 commission falls below 0.5% of the trade once the portfolio exceeds about US$68,000. Below that the commission share is higher but stays a fixed US$1.70.
- The month-end check is the enforcement point. Price moves between month-ends can carry B above 5% for up to a month, and this is disclosed to the owner.
- A bitcoin sale is never made because of drawdown, news or volatility (S0). The trim is a fixed owner limit, so it is exempt from the cooling-off period and the drawdown guardrail.
- If the owner sets a 4.5% target, buys stop at 4.5%, the trim still fires above 5.0%, and it sells to 4.5%.

S8. US-situs cap. At a month-end with V_us above US$60,000 (or the cap as updated after EVT06), switch the excess, bringing V_us down to US$54,000, into the same-group non-US line within one month: a UCITS line, or an LSE bitcoin ETP for bitcoin. Lines with the largest withholding gap go first, split across sessions under the CST06 size rule. If no eligible non-US line exists, sell the excess and route it by FIT01 gaps. From US$54,000 no new US-listed buys are allowed (FIT12). S8 is a legal-exposure switch with the same exposure and no market timing, so it is exempt from the drawdown guardrail and the cooling-off period.

S9. Information-only products (ELG03, now including SVOL, YMAX, ULTY and BTCI) and excluded wrappers (ELG01: US partnerships, unsecured ETNs) are never bought. If one is found in the account, it is exited under S1.

Guardrails:
- a 30-day cooling-off and a re-run of the scores before any non-structural sale (S2, S3, S5);
- no non-structural sale while the world is more than 15% below its 52-week high (MAC09); S1, S6, S7 and S8 are exempt;
- missing, stale or unverified data never triggers a sale.
The core world line is sold only under S1, S2 or S8.
